[4mBASIC[24m(1)                    General Commands Manual                   [4mBASIC[24m(1)

[1mNAME[0m
       basic - extended integer BASIC interpreter

[1mSYNOPSIS[0m
       [1mbasic[0m

[1mDESCRIPTION[0m
       [4mBasic[24m  is  an interactive BASIC interpreter written in strict K&R C for
       the AT&T 3B2 and other System V systems.  It is an [4minteger[24m  BASIC:  all
       numbers  are  signed  32-bit  integers, and there is no floating point.
       Strings of up to 80 characters,  one-dimensional  arrays,  subroutines,
       and file save and load are supported.

       When started, the interpreter prints a banner and the prompt ``[1m? [22m''.  A
       line that begins with a line number is stored in the program; any other
       line  is  executed immediately.  Input is read from the standard input,
       so program text and commands may also be piped in.

       Keywords may be typed in upper or  lower  case.   Text  outside  double
       quotes is converted to upper case when a line is entered, so [1mLIST [22mshows
       keywords in upper case and string literals exactly as typed.

[1mCOMMANDS[0m
       These are recognized only at the prompt, not inside a program.

       [1mRUN         [22mClear  all  variables  and  run the program from its lowest
                   line number.  The random number  seed  is  not  reset  (see
                   [1mRND[22m).

       [1mLIST [4m[22m[n[-m]][0m
                   List  the  program.   [1mLIST  [22mlists every line, [1mLIST 50 [22mlists
                   line 50, [1mLIST 50-90 [22mlists lines 50  through  90,  [1mLIST  50-[0m
                   lists  from  line  50  to the end, and [1mLIST -90 [22mlists up to
                   line 90.

       [1mNEW         [22mDelete the program and clear all variables.

       [1mSAVE [4m[22m"file"[24m Write the program to [4mfile[24m as text, one  numbered  line  per
                   line.  Prints [1mSAVED[22m.

       [1mLOAD [4m[22m"file"[24m Delete  the  current program, then read numbered lines from
                   [4mfile[24m.  Prints [1mLOADED[22m.  The file  name  must  be  in  double
                   quotes.

       [1mBYE         [22mLeave the interpreter.  End-of-file (usually CTRL-D) at the
                   prompt does the same.

       Typing  a  line number followed by nothing deletes that line.  Typing a
       line number followed by text inserts or replaces that line.  Line  num‐
       bers may be any non-negative integer; lines are kept in order.

[1mVARIABLES AND TYPES[0m
       Variable names are a single letter.  There are 26 numeric variables [1mA[22m-[1mZ[0m
       and 26 string variables [1mA$[22m-[1mZ$[22m.  Arrays are one-dimensional: [1mA(n) [22mis nu‐
       meric and [1mA$(n) [22mis a string array.  Array subscripts run from 0 through
       the  declared  size.  An array that is used without being declared with
       [1mDIM [22mhas subscripts 0 through 10.  Scalars and arrays of the same letter
       are separate variables (for example [1mA [22mand [1mA(1)[22m).  Numeric variables and
       arrays start at 0 and string variables at the empty string.

       Numbers are signed 32-bit integers,  from  -2147483648  to  2147483647.
       Arithmetic that overflows wraps around silently.  To write the smallest
       value,  use  [1m-2147483647-1[22m.  Strings hold at most 80 characters; longer
       results are silently truncated.

[1mEXPRESSIONS[0m
       Operators, from highest precedence to lowest:

           ^                       exponentiation (right to left)
           unary -  unary +
           *  /  MOD               multiply, divide, remainder
           +  -                    add, subtract
           =  <>  <  >  <=  >=     comparison
           NOT
           AND
           OR

       Division truncates toward  zero.   [1mMOD  [22mtakes  the  sign  of  its  left
       operand.   A negative exponent yields 0 (1 if the base is 1).  Division
       or [1mMOD [22mby zero is an error.  Because unary minus ranks below [1m^[22m, [1m-2^2 [22mis
       -4.

       Comparisons yield -1 for true and 0 for false.  [1mAND[22m, [1mOR[22m, and [1mNOT  [22moper‐
       ate  bitwise  on  the 32-bit values, as in Microsoft BASIC.  This means
       [1mNOT 5 [22mis -6, which is non-zero and therefore true when tested by [1mIF[22m.

       Strings may be joined with [1m+[22m.  Two strings may be compared with [1m=[22m,  [1m<>[22m,
       [1m<[22m,  [1m>[22m, [1m<=[22m, and [1m>=[22m; the comparison is by character code.  Mixing strings
       and numbers in one expression is a [1mTYPE MISMATCH [22merror.

[1mSTATEMENTS[0m
       Several statements may be placed on one line, separated by colons.

       [1m[LET] [4m[22mvar[24m [1m= [4m[22mexpr[0m
              Assignment.  [1mLET [22mis optional.

       [1mPRINT [4m[22mlist[0m
              Print the items in [4mlist[24m,  which  are  expressions  separated  by
              semicolons  or  commas.   [1m?   [22mmay  be used in place of [1mPRINT[22m.  A
              semicolon prints the next item immediately  after  the  previous
              one.   A comma moves to the next 14-column print zone.  A trail‐
              ing semicolon or comma suppresses the  final  newline.   Numbers
              are printed with no leading or trailing blanks.

       [1mINPUT ["prompt"; or ,] [4m[22mvar[24m[1m[,[4m[22mvar[24m[1m...][0m
              Read  values  from  the  terminal.   With  a semicolon after the
              prompt, or no prompt, the prompt is followed by ``[1m? [22m''.  With  a
              comma  after  the  prompt, no question mark is printed.  Several
              variables may be answered on one line separated  by  commas;  if
              too  few values are given, ``[1m?? [22m'' asks for the rest.  A non-nu‐
              meric answer to a numeric variable prints [1m?REDO FROM  START  [22mand
              the  whole  [1mINPUT  [22mis  asked  again.  An empty numeric answer is
              taken as 0.  End-of-file stops the program.

       [1mIF [4m[22mcond[24m [1mTHEN [4m[22mstatement[24m [1m[ELSE [4m[22mstatement[24m[1m][0m

       [1mIF [4m[22mcond[24m [1mTHEN [4m[22mline[24m [1m[ELSE [4m[22mline[24m[1m][0m

       [1mIF [4m[22mcond[24m [1mGOTO [4m[22mline[0m
              Conditional execution.  [4mCond[24m is true if non-zero.  A line number
              after [1mTHEN [22mor [1mELSE [22mmeans [1mGOTO[22m.  Statements following the  chosen
              branch on the same line, separated by colons, are executed after
              it.

       [1mGOTO [4m[22mexpr[0m

       [1mGOSUB [4m[22mexpr[0m
              Jump  to,  or call as a subroutine, the line whose number is the
              value of [4mexpr[24m.  Computed targets such as [1mGOTO 100*N [22mare allowed.
              Typing [1mGOTO [22mat the prompt starts the program at that line.

       [1mRETURN [22mReturn from the most recent [1mGOSUB[22m.

       [1mFOR [4m[22mv[24m [1m= [4m[22ma[24m [1mTO [4m[22mb[24m [1m[STEP [4m[22mc[24m[1m][0m

       [1mNEXT [[4m[22mv[24m[1m[,[4m[22mv[24m[1m...]][0m
              Counting loop.  The step defaults to 1 and may be negative.  The
              body always executes at least once, even if the  starting  value
              is  already  past  the  limit,  as  in  classic Microsoft BASIC.
              Restarting a [1mFOR [22mon a variable that is already  active  discards
              that loop and any loops inside it.

       [1mDIM [4m[22mA[24m[1m([4m[22mn[24m[1m)[,[4m[22mB$[24m[1m([4m[22mn[24m[1m)...][0m
              Declare  arrays with subscripts 0 through [4mn[24m.  Numeric arrays may
              be up to size 5000 and string arrays up to  500.   Declaring  an
              existing array again discards its contents.

       [1mDATA [4m[22mitem[24m[1m[,[4m[22mitem[24m[1m...][0m

       [1mREAD [4m[22mvar[24m[1m[,[4m[22mvar[24m[1m...][0m

       [1mRESTORE[0m
              [1mDATA  [22mholds  numbers  and  quoted  strings.  [1mREAD [22mtakes the next
              items in program order, and [1mRESTORE [22mreturns to the  first  item.
              Reading  past  the  end  is  an [1mOUT OF DATA [22merror, and reading a
              string into a numeric variable or the reverse is a [1mTYPE MISMATCH[0m
              error.

       [1mREM [4m[22mtext[0m

       [1m' [4m[22mtext[24m A comment.  Everything to the end of the line is ignored, colons
              included.

       [1mRANDOMIZE [[4m[22mn[24m[1m][0m
              Set the random number seed to [4mn[24m, or to the time of day if  [4mn[24m  is
              omitted.

       [1mCLS    [22mSend a form feed to the terminal.

       [1mEND    [22mStop the program.

       [1mSTOP   [22mStop the program and print [1mBREAK IN [4m[22mline[24m.

       [1mGOSUB[22m, [1mRETURN[22m, [1mFOR[22m, and [1mNEXT [22mmay be used only inside a running program.
       At the prompt they produce [1m?ILLEGAL DIRECT[22m.

[1mFUNCTIONS[0m
       [1mRND([4m[22mn[24m[1m)        [22mA  pseudo-random  integer from 1 through [4mn[24m.  [4mn[24m must be at
                     least 1.  The generator starts from a  fixed  seed  every
                     time  [4mbasic[24m  is  started, so a program that does not call
                     [1mRANDOMIZE [22mproduces the same sequence each time.  The seed
                     carries over from one [1mRUN [22mto the next within a session.

       [1mSQR([4m[22mn[24m[1m)        [22mInteger square root, rounded down.  An error if [4mn[24m is neg‐
                     ative.

       [1mABS([4m[22mn[24m[1m)        [22mAbsolute value.

       [1mSGN([4m[22mn[24m[1m)        [22m-1, 0, or 1 according to the sign of [4mn[24m.

       [1mINT([4m[22mn[24m[1m)        [22mReturns [4mn[24m.  It is provided for compatibility,  since  all
                     values are already integers.

       [1mLEN([4m[22ms$[24m[1m)       [22mLength of a string.

       [1mASC([4m[22ms$[24m[1m)       [22mCharacter  code of the first character, or 0 for an empty
                     string.

       [1mVAL([4m[22ms$[24m[1m)       [22mThe integer at the start of the string;  0  if  there  is
                     none.

       [1mCHR$([4m[22mn[24m[1m)       [22mA  one-character  string whose code is the low eight bits
                     of [4mn[24m.

       [1mSTR$([4m[22mn[24m[1m)       [22mThe number as a string, with no leading blank.

       [1mLEFT$([4m[22ms$,n[24m[1m)   [22mThe first [4mn[24m characters.

       [1mRIGHT$([4m[22ms$,n[24m[1m)  [22mThe last [4mn[24m characters.

       [1mMID$([4m[22ms$,start[24m[1m[,[4m[22mlen[24m[1m])[0m
                     [4mLen[24m characters starting  at  position  [4mstart[24m,  where  the
                     first  character  is  position 1.  If [4mlen[24m is omitted, the
                     rest of the string is returned.

       [1mSPACE$([4m[22mn[24m[1m)     [22mA string of [4mn[24m blanks, 0 through 80.

[1mINTERRUPTING[0m
       The interrupt key (usually DEL or CTRL-C) stops a  running  program,  a
       [1mLIST  [22min  progress,  and prints [1mBREAK IN [4m[22mline[24m.  At the prompt it simply
       prints a new line and prompts again.  Variables are kept, so  the  pro‐
       gram can be examined or run again.

[1mDIAGNOSTICS[0m
       Errors  are reported as [1m?MESSAGE [22mand, when they occur in a running pro‐
       gram, [1m?MESSAGE IN [4m[22mline[24m[1m.  [22mA running program stops at  the  first  error;
       the interpreter returns to the prompt.

       [1mSYNTAX ERROR                [22mThe  statement  or  expression could not be
                                   understood, including a number with a deci‐
                                   mal point.

       [1mTYPE MISMATCH               [22mA string was used where a number is needed,
                                   or the reverse.

       [1mILLEGAL FUNCTION CALL       [22mAn argument is out of range: [1mRND  [22mbelow  1,
                                   [1mSQR [22mof a negative number, [1mMID$ [22mwith a start
                                   below  1  or  a  negative  length, [1mLEFT$ [22mor
                                   [1mRIGHT$ [22mwith a  negative  count,  or  [1mSPACE$[0m
                                   outside 0 to 80.

       [1mILLEGAL DIRECT              [22mA  statement  that  works only in a program
                                   was typed at the prompt.

       [1mDIVISION BY ZERO            [22mDivision or [1mMOD [22mby zero.

       [1mSUBSCRIPT OUT OF RANGE      [22mAn array index is negative or  larger  than
                                   the array size.

       [1mBAD SUBSCRIPT               [22mA  [1mDIM  [22msize  is  negative  or  exceeds the
                                   limit.

       [1mOUT OF MEMORY               [22mAn array or program line could not be allo‐
                                   cated.

       [1mMISSING ( [22mor [1mMISSING )      [22mA function call or parenthesized expression
                                   is not balanced.

       [1mUNDEFINED LINE [4m[22mn[24m            A [1mGOTO [22mor [1mGOSUB [22mrefers to a line that  does
                                   not exist.

       [1mRETURN WITHOUT GOSUB[0m

       [1mNEXT WITHOUT FOR            [22mControl structures are not matched.

       [1mGOSUB NESTING TOO DEEP[0m

       [1mFOR NESTING TOO DEEP        [22mMore  than 32 active [1mGOSUB[22ms or more than 16
                                   active [1mFOR[22ms.

       [1mOUT OF DATA                 READ [22mran past the last [1mDATA[22m.

       These messages are printed by the commands and are not program  errors:
       [1m?NEED QUOTED NAME [22m(a [1mSAVE [22mor [1mLOAD [22mwithout a file name in quotes), [1m?CAN‐[0m
       [1mNOT  OPEN  [4m[22mname[24m (the file could not be opened), [1m?PROGRAM TOO LONG [22m(more
       than 1000 lines), and [1m?REDO FROM START[22m, described under [1mINPUT[22m.

[1mLIMITS[0m
           Program lines                    1000
           Characters in an input line      255
           Characters in a string           80
           Numeric array subscripts         0 to 5000
           String array subscripts          0 to 500
           Active FOR loops                 16
           Active GOSUBs                    32
           Integer range                    -2147483648 to 2147483647

[1mEXAMPLES[0m
       A program that prints the squares of 1 through 5:

           10 FOR I = 1 TO 5
           20 PRINT I; " SQUARED IS "; I*I
           30 NEXT I
           RUN

       A subroutine called twice:

           10 A = 3: GOSUB 100
           20 A = 4: GOSUB 100
           30 END
           100 PRINT "SQR("; A; ") = "; SQR(A)
           110 RETURN

       Prompting for a string and a number:

           10 INPUT "Name"; N$
           20 INPUT "How many"; K
           30 FOR I = 1 TO K: PRINT "HELLO, "; N$: NEXT I

       Saving and reloading a program:

           SAVE "squares"
           NEW
           LOAD "squares"

       Running a program from a shell script:

           (cat squares.bas; echo RUN) | basic

[1mDIFFERENCES FROM MICROSOFT BASIC[0m
       There is no floating point: a decimal point is a syntax error, [1m/  [22mtrun‐
       cates,  and  functions  such as [1mSIN [22mand [1mLOG [22mdo not exist.  There are no
       multi-dimensional arrays, no [1mTAB[22m, no [1mINSTR[22m, no [1mON [4m[22mexpr[24m [1mGOTO[22m,  no  user-
       defined  functions,  and  no  multi-letter variable names.  [1mRND [22mtakes a
       limit and returns an integer rather than a fraction.  [1m^ [22massociates from
       right to left, so [1m2^3^2 [22mis 512.

[1mBUGS[0m
       Numbers outside the 32-bit range wrap silently when typed in answer  to
       [1mINPUT[22m, given to [1mVAL[22m, or written as a literal in a program.

       Redeclaring an array with [1mDIM [22mis not reported as an error.

       [1mCHR$ [22mdoes not report an out-of-range argument.

       [1mPRINT [22mprints the items before a syntax error in the same statement.

       With the standard System V terminal settings the characters [1m@ [22mand [1m# [22mare
       the  line-kill and erase characters and cannot be typed as ordinary in‐
       put without a preceding backslash.

